{"name":"Gold Infusion","hex":"#b1a57b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"},{"term":"infusion","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A product consisting of a liquid which has had other ingredients steeped in it to extract useful qualities.","The act of steeping or soaking a substance in liquid so as to extract medicinal or herbal qualities."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/infusion"}],"entries":[{"title":"Infusion","blocks":[{"text":"Infusion is the process of extracting chemical compounds or flavors from plant material in a solvent – such as water, oil, or alcohol –, by allowing the material to remain suspended in the solvent over time. \"Infusion\" is also the name for the resultant fluid mixture. The process of infusion is distinct from both decoction, a method of extraction involving boiling the plant material, and percolation, in which water is passed through the material."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/CoffeeBeansWhiteBackground.jpg/330px-CoffeeBeansWhiteBackground.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infusion","wikidata":"Q61951"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG/500px-A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG/1280px-A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG/500px-A_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_small_cup_of_coffee.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg/500px-Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg/1280px-Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg/500px-Gr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGr%C3%BCner_Mate_geschnitten.jpg"},{"srcse